<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Weimar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Weimar is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the residence of the dukes of Saxe-Weimar and later Saxe-Weimar-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a>. Around 1800 it became the centre of German literature, home to Goethe, Schiller, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottfried-herder">Herder</a> and Wieland. In 1919 the German National Assembly met there to draft a new constitution, giving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> its name, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauhaus">Bauhaus</a> school was founded in the city the same year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/weimar.jpg" alt="Weimar" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thuringius · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Weimar_stadtschloss_front_2025.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oldest records of Weimar date to 899, and its name derives from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-high-german">Old High German</a> words for holy and for standing water. It was the seat of the County of Weimar, first mentioned in 949, one of the most powerful jurisdictions in early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, and fell to the Wettins after 1346. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> was introduced in 1525, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> stayed in the city several times. After the Ernestines lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-war">Schmalkaldic War</a> in 1547 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertine-line">Albertines</a>, the ruler made Weimar his residence in 1552, and it remained one until the end of the monarchy in 1918.</p><p>The period from the regency of Anna Amalia, which began in 1758, through her son Carl August to Goethe's death in 1832 is called the golden or classical age. Goethe, Schiller, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottfried-herder">Herder</a>, Wieland and Bertuch lived there. Goethe moved to Weimar in 1775 and served the Grand Duke of Saxe-Weimar-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a> as Privy Councilor. A liberal Saxe-Weimar constitution came into effect in 1816. In 1842 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-liszt">Franz Liszt</a> became the Grand Ducal court conductor and organised the premiere of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-wagner">Richard Wagner</a>'s Lohengrin in 1850. Friedrich Nietzsche moved to the city in 1897 and died there three years later.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a>'s constitution was drafted in the city rather than in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> because the capital was considered too dangerous for the National Assembly after street rioting during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartacist-uprising">Spartacist uprising</a>. Reich President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-ebert">Friedrich Ebert</a> hoped the choice would remind the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> of Weimar Classicism while they deliberated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>. Artists and architects including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-gropius">Walter Gropius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wassily-kandinsky">Wassily Kandinsky</a> and Paul Klee founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauhaus">Bauhaus</a> there. The Nazis held a party convention in Weimar in 1926, and the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a> were set up near the city in 1933.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Weimar is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a>, with a population of about 65,000. It was the capital of Thuringia until 1948. Two World Heritage Sites include buildings in the city: Classical Weimar, with 11 sites linked to the 18th and early 19th centuries, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauhaus">Bauhaus</a> sites. The Bauhaus University, the Liszt School of Music and the Duchess Anna Amalia Library are among its institutions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Weimar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…1848 produced the Frankfurt Parliament in St Paul’s Church, whose Constitution of 28 March 1849 and its catalogue of basic rights later influenced the <b>Weimar</b> and post-war West German constitutions; the black, red and gold flag that the Diet itself adopted in 1848 became the German national colours.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Hitler never formally abolished the <b>Weimar</b> constitution: the Enabling Act of 1933 was renewed by the Reichstag in 1937 and 1939 and extended by decree in 1943.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The school’s own roots lay in the arts and crafts school founded in <b>Weimar</b> in 1906 by the Grand Duke of Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach and directed by the Belgian Art Nouveau architect Henry van de Velde.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bauhaus">Bauhaus · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>American soldiers arrived at Buchenwald near <b>Weimar</b>, where prisoners had already taken control of the camp.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-11">11 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After the 1662 division of Saxe-<b>Weimar</b>, Eisenach was briefly held by Adolf William, whose line ended in 1671.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-eisenach">Duchy of Saxe-Eisenach</a></li><li><blockquote>Saxe-Gotha was created in 1640 when the Ernestine lands of Saxe-<b>Weimar</b> were divided among the brothers of the ducal house, and Ernest I received Gotha.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-gotha">Duchy of Saxe-Gotha</a></li></ul></section>
